# rules: csharp-secret-from-a-predictable-source
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-338.cs
# from: CWE-338 (Cryptographically Weak PRNG), CWE-330
#
# `System.Random` is a subtractive generator seeded, by default, from the
# system clock. It is fast, it is repeatable, and it was never meant to be
# unguessable: given a few outputs the rest of the sequence follows, and given
# the time the process started so does the whole of it. A password reset token,
# a session id or a key drawn from it is guessable by anyone who can guess when
# it was made.
#
# The fix is one line - `RandomNumberGenerator` in modern .NET,
# `RNGCryptoServiceProvider` before it - which is why this is worth reporting
# even though `System.Random` is also the right tool for the many things that
# are not secrets.
#
# And that is the whole difficulty. A rule that reports every `new Random()`
# reports jitter, sampling, shuffling a carousel and picking a placeholder
# colour, which is most uses of it in any codebase, and gets turned off. So
# this one asks what the value is called: the name of the method it is used in,
# or the name it is assigned to, has to say the result is a secret - token,
# password, key, salt, nonce, session, otp, secret.
#
# It asks that of the names and not of the method's body, which is where the
# first draft asked it and was wrong. A body's text runs from brace to brace
# and includes the comments, so a rule guarded on the body matches any method
# with the word `secret` written in a comment - including, in the fixture, the
# `// ok:` annotation saying the rule must not fire there. A guard that reads
# comments is a guard on prose.
#
# That is a guess, and it is worth being honest about which way it errs. It
# misses a secret drawn from `System.Random` into a name that says nothing -
# `var v = new Random().Next()` returned as an id - and it will report a method
# called `TokenColour` that picks a colour. The first is the cost of not being
# turned off; the second is rare enough to be worth it. The alternative, a rule
# that fires on every use, is one nobody keeps.

["$RT $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| ["$T $V = new Random($$$_);"] as $assigned
| ["new Random($$$_)"] as $made
| "(?i)token|password|passwd|pwd|secret|key|salt|nonce|session|otp|credential|apikey|auth" as $namesASecret
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $methods + $assigned + $made) as $all
| ($all | of($made))
| within( ($all | of($methods) | where_capture("M"; $namesASecret))
          + ($all | of($assigned) | where_capture("V"; $namesASecret)) )
| finding("csharp-secret-from-a-predictable-source";
    "System.Random is seeded from the clock and its sequence follows from any "
    + "few outputs, so a secret drawn from it is guessable; "
    + "RandomNumberGenerator is the one that is not")
| report
